Not code unclarity — the finding is a restatement of the code's own comment, minus its first clause.

At `4471dad2e` (the commit reviewed), the arm read:

```go
case strategy.SyncRemoteSourceDefault, strategy.SyncRemoteSourceSole, strategy.SyncRemoteSourceFirst:
    // Sole is listed for exhaustiveness only and cannot occur here: the
    // caller writes this note solely when two or more remotes are
    // unpinned, and the resolver reaches the sole tier only with exactly
    // one remote configured. If that gate ever loosens, sole needs its own
    // wording — "your first push takes it over" is meaningless when there
    // is nothing to take it from.
```

The finding says: with `SyncRemoteSourceSole` the wording is meaningless because there's nothing to displace. That's the comment's *conditional* second half (`If that gate ever loosens…`) reported as a present defect, with the "cannot occur here" part dropped. So the reviewer picked up the hazard the comment flagged but not the guard the same comment documented — the classic switch-arm-read-in-isolation miss: `SyncRemoteSourceSole` appears in the `case` list, and the gate that makes it unreachable lives in the caller (`len(names) > 1` at `:196`) plus the `ambiguous()` early return.

Two contributing factors, if you want something actionable:

- **Listing `Sole` in a shared arm invites the reading.** The alternative is a separate `case strategy.SyncRemoteSourceSole:` that panics or writes the correct single-remote sentence — then the arm is self-evidently either impossible or handled, with no prose needed. That's a real tradeoff against the comment's own reason for sharing (the two sides count remotes with different git commands, so asserting impossibility is risky).
- **No test pins the unreachability.** Nothing fails if the gate loosens; the guarantee lives only in a comment, which is exactly the kind of thing a reviewer discounts.

`f9eb943f3` softened the claim to "all but unreachable" and named why (the two counts come from different git commands) — which is more honest but also weaker, so a future reviewer can file the same finding again. A test asserting the note is silent with one remote would close it off for good.

